= Quadratic fields, Pell equations and cyclotomic factors <app-quadratic>

@ch-diophantine studies the equation $b(b + x) = x^n + c$. Completing the square turns it into $U^2 = 4 x^n + x^2 + 4c$ with $U = 2b + x$, and the natural place to factor $U^2 - (x^2 + 4c)$ is a quadratic field. The same chapter explains the solution $(1, 7, 1)$ in base 904 by a special factorization of $7^7 + 1$. This appendix collects the algebraic number theory behind both. Marcus @marcus1977 and Ireland and Rosen @ireland-rosen1990 are good textbooks; Niven, Zuckerman and Montgomery @niven-zuckerman-montgomery1991 cover continued fractions; Cohen @cohen1993 covers the computations with PARI @pari2024.

== Quadratic fields and their integers <sec-app-quadratic-fields>

#definition(id: "app-quadratic-def-field", title: "Quadratic field")[
  For a squarefree integer $D != 0, 1$, the quadratic field $K = QQ(sqrt(D))$ is the set of numbers $alpha = a + c sqrt(D)$ with rational $a, c$. The conjugate of $alpha$ is $alpha' = a - c sqrt(D)$. The norm and the trace are
  $ N(alpha) = alpha alpha' = a^2 - D c^2, quad "Tr"(alpha) = alpha + alpha' = 2a . $
  The norm is multiplicative: $N(alpha beta) = N(alpha) N(beta)$.
]

The ring of integers $cal(O)_K$ is the set of elements of $K$ that are roots of a monic polynomial with integer coefficients. It is $ZZ[sqrt(D)]$ if $D equiv 2, 3 med (mod 4)$ and $ZZ[(1 + sqrt(D)) slash 2]$ if $D equiv 1 med (mod 4)$. Its discriminant is $4D$ or $D$. A subring of finite index, such as $ZZ[sqrt(D)]$ when $D equiv 1 med (mod 4)$, is called an order. The library works in orders like $ZZ[(x + sqrt(x^2 + 4c)) slash 2]$, whose discriminant $x^2 + 4c$ need not be squarefree (#lib("20.4")).

== Units and the Pell equation <sec-app-quadratic-units>

A unit is an element of $cal(O)_K$ whose inverse is also in $cal(O)_K$; equivalently, its norm is $plus.minus 1$. For a real quadratic field ($D > 0$) the units are exactly the numbers $plus.minus epsilon^k$, $k in ZZ$, for one fundamental unit $epsilon > 1$. Its logarithm $mu_0 = ln epsilon$ is the regulator.

Units are solutions of the Pell equation $u^2 - D v^2 = plus.minus 1$ (or $plus.minus 4$ in the other ring). They can be found with continued fractions. Write $sqrt(D) = [a_0; a_1, a_2, dots]$, a periodic continued fraction, and let $p_j slash q_j$ be its convergents.

#theorem(id: "app-quadratic-legendre", title: "Legendre", status: "cited")[
  If $alpha$ is irrational and $abs(alpha - p slash q) < 1 slash (2 q^2)$ with $q >= 1$, then $p slash q$ is a convergent of $alpha$. In particular, if $u, v > 0$ are coprime and $abs(u^2 - D v^2) < sqrt(D)$, then $u slash v$ is a convergent of $sqrt(D)$.
]

See @niven-zuckerman-montgomery1991[Theorem 7.24]. The second statement turns questions about small norms into a finite check along one period of the continued fraction.

#example(title: [The unit of the family $(1, x, 1)$])[
  For odd $x$, the number $epsilon = (x + sqrt(x^2 + 4)) slash 2$ is a unit of norm $(x^2 - (x^2 + 4)) slash 4 = -1$ and trace $x$. For every integer $b$, $N(b + epsilon) = b^2 + b x - 1$. So the equation $b(b + x) = x^n + 1$ says exactly $N(b + epsilon) = x^n$: an element of prescribed norm, in a field that changes with $x$ (@sec-dio-field). When $E = (x slash 2)^2 + 1$ for even $x$, the continued fraction is $sqrt(E) = [x slash 2; x, x, x, dots]$, every convergent has norm $plus.minus 1$, and Legendre's theorem shows that no element has a small norm such as $plus.minus 2^j$ (Lemma 20.16 of #lib("20.3")).
]

Fields of Richaud-Degert type, $D = a^2 + r$ with $r | 4a$, have a fundamental unit of size about $2a$, as in the example. For most real quadratic fields the fundamental unit is much larger, of size up to $e^(sqrt(D))$. This difference decides whether the methods of @sec-dio-baker work (#lib("20.4")).

== Ideals and the class group <sec-app-quadratic-ideals>

Unique factorization of elements can fail in $cal(O)_K$. The classic example is $6 = 2 dot 3 = (1 + sqrt(-5))(1 - sqrt(-5))$ in $ZZ[sqrt(-5)]$. Ideals repair this.

#theorem(id: "app-quadratic-ideals", title: "Unique factorization of ideals", status: "cited")[
  Every nonzero ideal of $cal(O)_K$ is a product of prime ideals in exactly one way. An odd prime $p$ splits as $p cal(O)_K = P P'$ with $P != P'$ when the discriminant is a nonzero square modulo $p$, stays prime when it is a nonsquare, and ramifies as $P^2$ when $p$ divides the discriminant.
]

The norm of an ideal $I$ is the size of $cal(O)_K slash I$, and $N(alpha cal(O)_K) = abs(N(alpha))$. Two ideals are equivalent if one is a nonzero element times the other. The classes form a finite abelian group, the class group, whose order $h$ is the class number. An ideal is principal, that is generated by one element, exactly when its class is trivial. If $B^n$ is principal, the order $d$ of the class of $B$ divides $n$.

#example(title: "Why ideals enter the equation")[
  For odd $x$ every prime $p | x$ splits in $QQ(sqrt(x^2 + 4))$, since $x^2 + 4 equiv 4$ is a square modulo $p$. A solution of $N(b + epsilon) = x^n$ gives $(b + epsilon) = B^n$ for an ideal $B$ of norm $x$ that takes one prime above each $p | x$. If $x$ is a prime power, $B$ is determined up to conjugation by the explicit element $epsilon + 1$, which has norm $x$ ("pure" solutions). Otherwise $B$ can mix the primes, and its class need not be trivial ("mixed" solutions). The case that remains open for $b(b + x) = x^x + 1$ is a mixed $B$ whose class has a large order dividing $x$ (@cor-x-x).
]

For real quadratic fields the class number and the regulator are tied by the class number formula,
$ h dot mu_0 = sqrt(Delta) / 2 dot L(1, chi), $
where $Delta$ is the discriminant and $L(1, chi)$ is the value at 1 of the $L$-function of the quadratic character $chi$. The value $L(1, chi)$ is of moderate size, roughly between $Delta^(-epsilon)$ and $ln Delta$. So a small regulator forces a large class number, and the reverse. In $QQ(sqrt(x^2 + 4))$ the regulator is at most $ln x$ or so, and the class number can be as large as about $x slash ln x$, which is why the class group conditions of @cor-x-x cannot be excluded by size alone (#lib("20.5")).

== Cyclotomic polynomials, Gauss sums and Ramanujan sums <sec-app-quadratic-cyclotomic>

The cyclotomic polynomial $Phi_m (t)$ is the monic polynomial whose roots are the primitive $m$-th roots of unity. It has integer coefficients and degree $phi(m)$, and $t^n - 1 = product_(d | n) Phi_d (t)$. For odd $x$,
$ x^x + 1 = product_(d | x) Phi_(2d) (x), $
so $x^x + 1 = (x + 1) Phi_(2x) (x)$ when $x$ is prime.

Two exponential sums appear in the proofs of @sec-dio-aurifeuille. Let $zeta$ be a primitive $m$-th root of unity.

- The Ramanujan sum $c_m (j) = sum_(a) zeta^(a j)$, over the residues $a$ prime to $m$. It is an integer, $c_m (j) = sum_(d | gcd(j, m)) mu(m slash d) d$ with the Moebius function $mu$, and $abs(c_m (j)) <= gcd(j, m)$.
- The Gauss sum of a character $chi$ modulo $m$, $tau(chi) = sum_a chi(a) zeta^a$. For the primitive quadratic character of a real quadratic field with discriminant $Delta$, $tau(chi) = sqrt(Delta)$. The twisted sums $sum_a chi(a) zeta^(a j)$ are $chi(j) tau(chi)$ when $j$ is prime to $m$ and 0 otherwise.

See @ireland-rosen1990[chapter 6] and @washington1997.

== Aurifeuillian factorizations <sec-app-quadratic-aurifeuille>

Some values of cyclotomic polynomials split for an algebraic reason. The oldest example is
$ 2^(4k + 2) + 1 = (2^(2k+1) - 2^(k+1) + 1)(2^(2k+1) + 2^(k+1) + 1). $
In general, for squarefree $x equiv 3 med (mod 4)$ the polynomial $Phi_(2x) (x t^2)$ factors over $ZZ$ into two polynomials of degree $phi(x)$, and setting $t = 1$ splits $Phi_(2x)(x)$ into two Aurifeuillian factors $L < M$. The reason is that $sqrt(x)$ lies in the field of $4x$-th roots of unity, so the roots split into two halves that the Galois group of $QQ(zeta)$ over $QQ(sqrt(x))$ preserves. Brent @brent1993 and Stevenhagen @stevenhagen1987 give formulas for the factors.

#example(title: "The factors behind base 904")[
  For $x = 3$: $Phi_6 (3 t^2) = 9 t^4 - 3 t^2 + 1 = (3t^2 + 3t + 1)(3t^2 - 3t + 1)$, which gives $Phi_6 (3) = 7 = 7 dot 1$. Then $3^3 + 1 = 4 dot 1 dot 7$, and $b = 4 dot 1 = 4$, $b + 3 = 7$ solve $b(b + 3) = 3^3 + 1$: this is $(1, 3, 1)$ in base 4.

  For $x = 7$: $Phi_(14) (7) = 102943 = 113 dot 911$, an Aurifeuillian split with $L = 113$ and $M = 911$. Then $7^7 + 1 = 823544 = 8 dot 113 dot 911$, and $b = 8 dot 113 = 904$, $b + 7 = 911$ solve $b(b + 7) = 7^7 + 1$: this is $(1, 7, 1)$ in base 904. It works because $M slash L = 8.06$ is close to $x + 1 = 8$. The library proves that $M slash L -> e^2 approx 7.39$ as $x$ grows (@thm-aurifeuille), so the coincidence cannot repeat for larger $x$.
]

The proof of @thm-aurifeuille writes $ln(M slash L)$ as a sum over the roots in one half, expands $ln(1 plus.minus zeta^a slash sqrt(x))$ in powers of $x^(-1 slash 2)$, and evaluates the resulting sums of roots of unity with the Ramanujan and Gauss sums above.

== Two reformulations of $b(b + x) = x^x + 1$ <sec-app-quadratic-forms>

These illustrate how the tools above combine. Both are proven in #lib("16.2").

- The $x$-adic form. Let $x$ be an odd prime. The polynomial $t^2 + x t - 1$ has the simple root $t equiv 1$ modulo $x$, so by Hensel's lemma (@app-padic) it has a root $beta in ZZ_x$ with $beta equiv 1$. Let $R$ be the residue of $beta$ modulo $x^x$, in $[0, x^x)$. Then the equation has a solution exactly when $R < x^(x slash 2)$, and then $b = R$. For $x = 7$, $R = 904$ and $7^(3.5) approx 907.5$. For a random residue this happens with probability about $x^(-x slash 2)$.
- The unit form. Let $x equiv 3 med (mod 4)$ be squarefree with $x^2 + 4$ prime, and $epsilon$ the fundamental unit of $ZZ[sqrt(x)]$. A solution gives $(2b + x) + 2 x^((x-1) slash 2) sqrt(x) = plus.minus (x + 2 plus.minus 2 sqrt(x)) epsilon^k$. For $x = 7$: $1815 + 686 sqrt(7) = (9 + 2 sqrt(7))(8 + 3 sqrt(7))^2$. So the equation asks whether one coordinate of the sequence $gamma epsilon^k$ ever equals $2 x^((x-1) slash 2)$. For fixed $x$ this is a problem for linear forms in logarithms (@app-baker). The difficulty is that the field changes with $x$.
